  translation: "1. The blessed Lord said: I shall declare to you, who are free of spite, this most secret knowledge joined with realization. Knowing it, you will be released from what is impure.\n2. It is the king of knowledges and the king of secrets, this highest purifier, grasped directly, righteous, very easy to practice, and imperishable.\n3. Men without faith in this teaching, O scorcher of foes, do not reach me, and they remain on the path of the world that ends in death.\n4. By me, whose form is unmanifest, this whole world is pervaded. All beings abide in me, and I am not situated in them.\n5. Nor do beings abide in me. See my divine yoga. Supporting beings, and not standing in them, my self brings beings forth.\n6. As the vast wind, going everywhere, always stands in the sky, so understand that all beings abide in me.\n7. All beings, O son of Kuntī, enter my nature at the close of a kalpa, and at a kalpa's beginning I send them forth again.\n8. Taking hold of my own nature, I send forth again and again this whole host of beings, which is not its own master, under the sway of nature.\n9. And those actions do not bind me, O winner of wealth, as I sit like one indifferent, unattached among those actions.\n10. With me as its witness, nature brings forth what moves and what is still. By this cause, O son of Kuntī, the world turns through its changes.\n11. The deluded scorn me when I have taken a human body. They do not know my supreme state, the great Lord of beings.\n12. Their hopes are vain, their works are vain, their knowledge is vain, and their judgment is gone. They have taken to a deluding nature, the nature of rākṣasas and of asuras.\n13. But the great-souled, O Pārtha, having taken to a divine nature, worship me with their minds on no other, knowing me as the imperishable source of beings.\n14. Always praising me, striving, and firm in their vow, and bowing to me with devotion, those who are constantly engaged worship me.\n15. Others, too, worship me by the sacrifice that is knowledge: as the one, as the many distinct forms, and in many ways as the all-faced.\n16. I am the Vedic rite, I am the sacrifice, I am the offering to the ancestors, and I am the food all beings eat. I am the mantra, I alone am the offering of ghee, I am the fire, and I am the act of offering.\n17. I am the father of this world, the mother, the ordainer, and the grandfather. I am what is to be known, the purifier, the syllable oṃ, and the Ṛg, the Sāman, and the Yajus.\n18. I am the fruit of action, the nourisher, the master, the witness, the dwelling, the refuge, and the friend. I am the arising, the dissolution, the abiding, the store, and the imperishable seed.\n19. I heat, and I hold the rain back and release it. I am the deathless, and death as well, and I am what is and what is not, O Arjuna.\n20. Knowers of the three Vedas, the soma-drinkers, whose sins are cleansed, worship me with sacrifices and pray for the way to heaven. Reaching the fruit of their merit, they enjoy divine pleasures in heaven, in Indra's world.\n21. Having enjoyed that vast heaven, they enter the world of mortals when their merit is exhausted. Thus those who follow the rite of the three Vedas, desiring desires, get only the going and the coming.\n22. Those men who are not other think of me and worship me wholly. For them, constantly engaged, I myself carry what they have yet to gain and the keeping of it.\n23. Even those who are devoted to other gods and worship them with faith worship me alone, O son of Kuntī, though ignorance goes before that worship.\n24. For I am the enjoyer of every sacrifice, and its lord. But they do not know me as I really am, and so they fall from the fruit of the sacrifice.\n25. Those whose vow is the gods go to the gods, and those whose vow is the fathers go to the fathers. Worshippers of the spirits go to the spirits, and those who worship me come to me.\n26. Whoever offers me a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water with devotion, I accept that, brought with devotion, from the one whose mind is pure.\n27. Whatever you do, whatever you eat, whatever you offer, whatever you give, and whatever austerity you practice, O son of Kuntī, make that an offering to me.\n28. Offering them in this way, you will be freed from actions whose fruits are wanted and unwanted, the very bonds. With your mind yoked to that yoga of renunciation, and freed, you will come to me.\n29. I am the same in all beings. None is hateful to me, and none is dear. But those who worship me with devotion are in me, and I am in them.\n30. Even if a man of very vile conduct worships me with no other devotion, he is to be held a man of right conduct, for his resolve is set on the good.\n31. He quickly becomes one whose mind is dharma, and he reaches lasting peace. Promise this, O son of Kuntī: my devotee does not perish.\n32. For even those of low birth who take refuge in me, O Pārtha — women, vaiśyas, and śūdras — they too go to the highest goal.\n33. How much more, then, brāhmaṇas of meritorious birth, devotees, and royal seers. Having reached this impermanent, joyless world, worship me.\n34. Be one whose mind is on me, my devotee, one who worships me, and bow to me. Having yoked the self thus, and holding me as your highest resort, you will come to me alone.",
